{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_44","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n \\\"entryId|position|message_end|source position|monotonic|AgentMessage|turnIndex\\\" docs/specs/02-pi-lhc docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes packages/pi-lhc -g '*.md' -g '*.ts' -g '*.json'\"","aggregated_output":"docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/00-prd.md:266:**AC-3.2:** Compact is invocable with a named profile or explicit parameters, returning a receipt (band composition, gaps, what was requeued). Health and sweep commands expose LHC's reports and repair actions; repair distinguishes recoverable failures (requeue helps) from config-blocked failures (requeue futile until the named fix).\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/00-prd.md:386:### Extension decomposition\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:50:  \"message_end\",\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:127: *  (message_end is a no-op until Story 2) and lands no work handlers, so no\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:346:  function fallbackIdFor(kind: string, position: number | undefined): string | undefined {\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:347:    return position !== undefined ? `${kind}:${position}` : undefined;\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:352:    position: number | undefined,\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:358:      fallbackIdFor(hook, position) ?? `${hook}:no-source-position`,\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:375:  // message_end: map the finalized PI message to ordered LHC events, track the\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:377:  const onMessageEnd: PiHookHandler<\"message_end\"> = async (_ctx, event) => {\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:380:      event.entryId !== undefined && event.entryId !== \"\"\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:381:        ? { piSessionId: captureSession.piSessionId, entryId: event.entryId }\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:384:            fallbackId: fallbackIdFor(\"message_end\", event.position),\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:390:      await recordMappingFailure(\"message_end\", event.position, cause);\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:404:      fallbackId: fallbackIdFor(\"model_select\", event.position),\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:414:      fallbackId: fallbackIdFor(\"thinking_level_select\", event.position),\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:463:    message_end: onMessageEnd as PiHookHandler<Epic1Hook>,\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/post-epic-4-work.md:141:Validated in the POC (commits `1cf2dc45`, `6a9aa7a4`, `f12a850d`) and confirmed by two sessions of live usage: cache-invalidation cost from tool-result trimming dropped to roughly 10–20% of what it was. Epic 3 as built already has most of the design — monotonic boundary, max/target hysteresis, whole-message floor, summary-with-truncation-fallback, compact reset. What it doesn't have is the turn-end trigger: Epic 3's advance check runs after every intake batch commits (message-end granularity, the pre-experiment design, AC-4.9). The turn-end refinement came after Epic 3's stories were accepted and was validated POC-side only; it lives in no spec yet.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:16:| `message_end` | finalized `user` / `assistant` / `toolResult` message | Map → intake event batch → `sdk.intakeStream.messageEvents` |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:17:| `turn_end` | `turnIndex`, `message`, `toolResults` | Emit `turn_end` intake event (closes LHC turn, triggers boundary advance + derivations) |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:18:| `context` | deep copy of `messages`, return `{ messages }` to replace | Serve the thread view: `sdk.threadView.pull()` → map to `AgentMessage[]` |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:36:- `message_end` handlers may replace the message; other extensions' mutations are visible — extension ordering matters if anything else mutates context.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:37:- Parallel tool mode: `tool_execution_*` events interleave, but final `toolResult` `message_end` events still arrive in assistant source order — intake ordering is safe if we drive everything off `message_end`.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:41:The POC (`pi-extension.ts`, ~2,900 lines) wires: `session_start` (snapshot ctx, reconcile rollout, hydrate truncation state), `message_end` (capture → side-channel tool-result restore → schedule smoothing → buffer truncation observation), `tool_result` (stash original content before PI/extension truncation), `turn_end` (finalize turn, parallel intake, observe/refresh projection, schedule background maintenance), `agent_end` (refresh projection), `context` (apply prompt-visible truncation), plus ~12 `lh-*` commands.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:44:- The event-to-record mapper (`pi-message-mapper.ts`) is a complete worked example of PI message → canonical mapping: role→kind mapping, content-part fan-out (text/thinking/toolCall/image/fileRef/unknown), idempotency key construction (`pi:<session>:entry:<entryId>`, responseId/toolCallId fallbacks, content fingerprint last resort), token telemetry extraction from assistant `usage`. The new converter reimplements this against `MessageEventInput` (which is far simpler — 7 event kinds, text-only payloads).\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:47:- Side-channel capture of original tool results before truncation (`tool_result` event fires before `message_end`).\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:64:message_end ────────────────────► map message → event batch ────► intakeStream.messageEvents(ref, events)\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:68:context ────────────────────────► pull + map ───────────────────► threadView.pull(ref) → ViewMessage[] → AgentMessage[]\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:79:**Event mapping (the converter contract):** PI `message_end` fans out to `MessageEventInput`s:\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:86:**Context serving (the mapping back):** `ViewMessage { role: \"user\"|\"assistant\", content }` → PI `AgentMessage`. Band messages are plain user-role text blocks; tail messages are pre-rendered text (tool calls/results already formatted by LHC's mapping table in `shared/view.ts:60-77`). Two things to verify in the dial-in/recording period: whether serving tool calls as flattened text (vs. native `toolCall` content parts) affects model behavior, and what PI requires of the first/last message roles.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:115:  → turn_start{turnIndex}\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:116:     → message_start/message_end (user [text])\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:117:     → message_start/message_end (assistant [thinking?, text?, toolCall×N])\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:119:     → message_start/message_end (toolResult [text]) ×N\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:120:     → turn_end{turnIndex, message, toolResults[]}\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:121:  → turn_start{turnIndex+1} ... (a new turn per agent step until no tool calls)\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:127:- **Parallel tool calls.** All N calls arrive in *one* assistant `message_end` (content = `[toolCall, toolCall, toolCall]`), each `{type,id,name,arguments}`. `tool_execution_start` events interleave and `tool_result`s complete out of order, but each `toolResult` `message_end` carries its `toolCallId`, so ordering is recoverable by ID, not arrival. Fan-out to LHC `tool_call` events is per-content-part, exactly as the mapper assumed.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:130:- **Resume/dedup — the big one.** Reopening an existing session (`--session-id` of a prior session) does **NOT** re-fire historical `message_end` events. Only the new turn's events fire (verified: 4 message events, not 10). PI loads prior history internally and exposes it through the **`context` hook** (first context on resume already held all 7 prior messages). **Consequence: LHC intake never sees duplicate historical events on resume — the dedup concern is far smaller than feared.** Idempotency keys still matter for `/reload` and crash-replay, but normal resume is clean by construction.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:131:- **Assistant message composition.** Confirmed `[thinking, text]`, `[thinking, toolCall]`, `[toolCall×N]` all occur; thinking carries an encrypted `thinkingSignature` (provider reasoning token — opaque, store-or-drop is an LHC call). The fan-out order in one message is thinking → text → toolCalls.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:136:**Hard-kill abort, captured solo:** `kill -9` mid-stream left the log at `session_start → before_agent_start → agent_start → turn_start → message_end(user)` — **no assistant `message_end`, no `turn_end`, no `agent_end`, no `session_shutdown`.** The incomplete-turn shape: user prompt captured, no agent response, turn never closed. LHC reattach after a crash finds an open turn with a trailing user prompt; intake must tolerate it. This is the crash-safety shape AC-1.4 needs.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:149:- **Graceful interrupt (Esc):** the partial assistant message gets a normal `message_end` with content preserved (`[thinking, text]`, ~120 lines kept) and **`stopReason: \"aborted\"`**; `turn_end` fires, also `stopReason: \"aborted\"`; `agent_end` fires. A graceful abort is a *complete, well-formed turn marked aborted* — partial content is real and worth keeping. The session continues normally on the next prompt (verified: follow-up ran `stopReason: \"stop\"`, no recovery path).\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:150:- **Hard-kill (`kill -9`, §5a):** nothing closes — user `message_end` only, no assistant close, no `turn_end`. Dangling open turn.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:153:**`turnIndex` is per-agent-run, not session-monotonic.** Each prompt's agent run resets `turn_start turn=0` and `agent_end` reports only that run's messages. The mapper cannot use PI `turnIndex` as a session-wide counter; session order comes from the session file's `parentId` chain. (Reinforces the §5a finding that PI turns ≠ LHC turns.)\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:163:- **Resume re-fires zero `message_end` events** (confirmed interactively, matching §5a) — history served via `context` only. Dedup is needed for `/reload` + crash-replay, not normal resume.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:193:**Epic B — Context serving:** ~4–5 stories. The `context` hook path (pull → AgentMessage mapping), PI compaction interception, materialize fallback command, mid-turn/abort edge behavior, cache-stability verification (the whole point — byte-stable prefixes between compacts).\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/post-epic-4-work.md:141:Validated in the POC (commits `1cf2dc45`, `6a9aa7a4`, `f12a850d`) and confirmed by two sessions of live usage: cache-invalidation cost from tool-result trimming dropped to roughly 10–20% of what it was. Epic 3 as built already has most of the design — monotonic boundary, max/target hysteresis, whole-message floor, summary-with-truncation-fallback, compact reset. What it doesn't have is the turn-end trigger: Epic 3's advance check runs after every intake batch commits (message-end granularity, the pre-experiment design, AC-4.9). The turn-end refinement came after Epic 3's stories were accepted and was validated POC-side only; it lives in no spec yet.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-tech-arch.md:80:| Event Capture | PI message/turn events → LHC intake batches (the converter, productionized); ordering, idempotency, dedup | PI `message_end`/`turn_end`, LHC intakeStream | Epic 1. The one mapper. No other surface writes intake events. |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-tech-arch.md:172:    SERVE->>SERVE: map ViewMessage[] → AgentMessage[]\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-tech-arch.md:179:3. The view is mapped to PI's `AgentMessage[]` shape and returned as the replacement.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-tech-arch.md:184:- **Transport:** PI `message_end` and `turn_end` events → Event Capture maps each to one or more LHC `MessageEventInput`s → `intakeStream.messageEvents(ref, batch)`.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-tech-arch.md:240:- **What ls-tech-design decides:** module decomposition within each surface, interface definitions, the message-kind mapping, mock construction and per-TC mapping, config schema, implementation sequences.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-tech-arch.md:244:The M0 working phase will surface facts — PI hook behavior, mapping realities, compaction control — that may revise decisions here (A7 especially). When it does, proceed with the better approach, document the deviation, and backfill this document. The tech arch is the starting position, not a decree.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:16:| `message_end` | finalized `user` / `assistant` / `toolResult` message | Map → intake event batch → `sdk.intakeStream.messageEvents` |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:17:| `turn_end` | `turnIndex`, `message`, `toolResults` | Emit `turn_end` intake event (closes LHC turn, triggers boundary advance + derivations) |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:18:| `context` | deep copy of `messages`, return `{ messages }` to replace | Serve the thread view: `sdk.threadView.pull()` → map to `AgentMessage[]` |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:36:- `message_end` handlers may replace the message; other extensions' mutations are visible — extension ordering matters if anything else mutates context.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:37:- Parallel tool mode: `tool_execution_*` events interleave, but final `toolResult` `message_end` event